{
  "id": "Compound::DB01203 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Nadolol is implicated in the pathogenesis of hypertension through a complex network of relationships involving beta-adrenergic antagonism. The evidence suggests that Nadolol, as a beta-blocker, is linked to Raynaud's Disease and Raynaud's Phenomenon, which are both caused by Propranolol or Metoprolol, both of which are also beta-blockers. This indicates a shared mechanism of action, where beta-blockers are associated with the treatment of hypertension. The lowest common ancestor of Nadolol and its related compounds, such as Propranolol and Metoprolol, points to a common pharmacological class—beta-adrenergic antagonists—highlighting the role of these drugs in modulating sympathetic nervous system activity. The integration of these pathways suggests that Nadolol may exert a therapeutic effect on hypertension through its beta-blocking properties, even though direct treatment evidence is not explicitly stated. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between beta-blockers and hypertension, emphasizing the importance of pharmacological classification in understanding drug-repurposing potential.",
